  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A sealed medical courier capsule of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest with an initial downward velocity of $4.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the capsule's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance acting on the capsule is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nFind the equation for $v(t).$ Express your answer in terms of time $t$ and acceleration due to gravity $g.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the capsule is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the capsule is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =9g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the capsule, we get\n\n$$\n9g=9\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.  We're told that the initial velocity of the capsule is $4.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=4.6$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n4.6=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=4.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the capsule $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+4.6.\n$$",
